{"id":"PHY-PCK-AVERAGE-SPEED-WEIGHTING-v1","caps":["PHY-CAP-AVERAGE-SPEED-EQUAL-DISTANCE"],"anchor":"For equal distances, the slower leg lasts longer, so a simple arithmetic mean of speeds has the wrong time weighting.","bridge":"Average speed is the one speed that covers the same total distance in the same total time.","validity":"Use total distance divided by total time; equal-distance and equal-time cases have different weight structures.","reconstruct":["assign equal leg distance d","derive each leg time d/v","sum total distance and total time","form the ratio before cancelling d"],"reps":["RATE_TABLE","DISTANCE_TIME_BARS","TOTAL_DISTANCE_TOTAL_TIME_RATIO"],"wrong":["using arithmetic mean for equal distances","assuming equal distance means equal time"],"contrast":"The same two speeds give different averages under equal-time versus equal-distance travel.","transfer":"unequal-distance legs and repeated cycles"},
{"id":"PHY-PCK-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION-MODEL-GATE-v1","caps":["PHY-CAP-MODEL-VALIDITY-CHECK","PHY-CAP-UNIFORM-ACCELERATION"],"anchor":"A kinematic equation is a compressed description of one model: acceleration must remain constant over the modeled interval.","bridge":"Ask whether constant acceleration is defensible before asking which formula contains the unknown.","validity":"Constant acceleration over the interval is a model gate; split phases when the model changes.","reconstruct":["identify the modeled interval","represent velocity change on a v-t graph","read constant slope as acceleration","derive state change before compressing to equations"],"reps":["MOTION_STRIP","VT_GRAPH","STATE_TABLE","EQUATION_FAMILY"],"wrong":["formula hunting from keywords","using one constant-a model across a phase change"],"contrast":"Identical known symbols do not justify the same equation if one motion has variable acceleration.","transfer":"piecewise acceleration with a model change","eq":["1/2","t^2","v^2"]},
{"id":"PHY-PCK-EQUATION-SELECTION-FIRST-MOVE-v1","caps":["PHY-CAP-FIRST-EQUATION","PHY-CAP-UNIFORM-ACCELERATION"],"anchor":"The first equation is a state-link chosen because it connects known states to the target without introducing an unnecessary unknown.","bridge":"Equation choice follows the known/target structure after model validity, not formula familiarity.","validity":"The constant-acceleration model must already be established.","reconstruct":["label u,v,a,t,s as known or unknown","name the target","choose a relation that avoids an extra unknown","say what that relation means physically","solve and verify"],"reps":["KNOWN_TARGET_STATE_TABLE","VT_GRAPH","KINEMATIC_RELATION_MAP"],"wrong":["choosing the most familiar formula","substituting before fixing signs"],"contrast":"Change only the requested quantity while keeping the same state data; the best first relation can change.","transfer":"stopping, speeding and unknown-time variants","eq":["1/2","t^2","v^2"]},
{"id":"PHY-PCK-STATE-PROPAGATION-PHASES-v1","caps":["PHY-CAP-STATE-PROPAGATE","PHY-CAP-MULTIPHASE-KINEMATICS","PHY-CAP-MOVING-RELEASE"],"anchor":"A new phase starts from the physical state at the end of the old phase; resetting an equation does not reset the body.","bridge":"Terminal state becomes next initial state unless a physical event creates a discontinuity.","validity":"State continuity depends on the boundary event; impulses can change velocity while ordinary release does not.","reconstruct":["mark the phase boundary","write terminal state","identify continuous variables","copy them into the next initial state","select the new phase model"],"reps":["PHASE_TIMELINE","BOUNDARY_STATE_TABLE","STATE_VECTOR_HANDOFF"],"wrong":["resetting phase-2 velocity to zero","carrying the old acceleration after the model changes"],"contrast":"A non-impulsive release changes the force model but not instantaneous velocity.","transfer":"moving-platform release and braking-then-coasting","eq":["sign changes","zero crossings"],"handoff":true},
{"id":"PHY-PCK-NTH-SECOND-INTERVAL-v1","caps":["PHY-CAP-NTH-SECOND-INTERVAL"],"anchor":"The nth second is the interval from n-1 to n, not the whole motion from zero to n.","bridge":"Displacement in the nth second is the cumulative displacement at n minus what had already accumulated at n-1.","validity":"The same motion model must cover both interval endpoints; distance requires extra care if direction reverses.","reconstruct":["write cumulative S(t)","evaluate S(n)","evaluate S(n-1)","subtract to isolate the interval"],"reps":["TIME_AXIS_INTERVAL","CUMULATIVE_S_T_CURVE","DIFFERENCE_BAR"],"wrong":["using S(n) as nth-second displacement","memorizing an nth-second formula without interval meaning"],"contrast":"S(4) includes the first three seconds; the fourth-second contribution is only S(4)-S(3).","transfer":"successive-second ratios and free-fall 1:3:5:7","eq":["nth-second subtraction","1/2","t^2"]},
